# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# EmailJS — powers the contact form in components/ContactForm.tsx
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The form sends directly from the browser via EmailJS (no backend needed).
# These keys are intentionally NEXT_PUBLIC_* — EmailJS's Public Key is
# designed to be exposed client-side, it is not a secret. The actual
# safeguards live in your EmailJS dashboard (see step 5 below).
#
# Setup:
#   1. Create a free account: https://dashboard.emailjs.com/sign-up
#   2. Email Services → Add New Service (e.g. Gmail) → copy its Service ID
#      → NEXT_PUBLIC_EMAILJS_SERVICE_ID
#   3. Email Templates → Create New Template. Set "To Email" to
#      luiszrita@gmail.com and use these variables in the template body:
#        {{from_email}}   — sender's address (also wired as Reply-To)
#        {{message}}      — the message body
#        {{to_email}}     — luiszrita@gmail.com (sent for convenience)
#      → copy the Template ID → NEXT_PUBLIC_EMAILJS_TEMPLATE_ID
#   4. Account → General → copy your Public Key
#      → NEXT_PUBLIC_EMAILJS_PUBLIC_KEY
#   5. Recommended hardening, both under Account → Security:
#      - Restrict allowed origins to your production domain (and
#        localhost while developing) so other sites can't reuse your keys.
#      - Enable the reCAPTCHA/hCaptcha integration to cut down on spam.
#      EmailJS's free tier is capped at 200 emails/month; the form also
#      throttles client-side (one submission per 20s) and uses a honeypot
#      field, but those only stop naive bots, not the dashboard-level
#      protections above.
#
# Copy this file to .env.local and fill in the three values below.
# .env.local is already git-ignored — never commit real keys.

NEXT_PUBLIC_EMAILJS_SERVICE_ID=
NEXT_PUBLIC_EMAILJS_TEMPLATE_ID=
NEXT_PUBLIC_EMAILJS_PUBLIC_KEY=
